<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Every burst has its second, its channel and its millisecond.</h1>
  <p class="lede">A skyBlip Go schedules each radio burst in three steps. Its serial number sets the seconds. Whether the second is even or odd sets the channel. A draw repeated every second sets the millisecond within that channel&rsquo;s window.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h3 id="second">The second, cut into three windows.</h3>
  <p class="lede">GPS marks the start of each UTC second. The Go has one radio, so it listens in three windows, each on a different channel. <%= link_to_page "radio", label: "The radio guide", anchor: "second" %> explains what it receives in each. Here, the question is when it transmits. All times below are measured from the start of the second.</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Window</th><th>Channel</th><th>Full listening window</th><th>The Go&rsquo;s own bursts start</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Uplink</th><td>869.525&nbsp;MHz</td><td>210 to 440&nbsp;ms</td><td>never: the ground station transmits here and the Go only listens</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Window 1</th><td>868.2&nbsp;MHz, channel A</td><td>450 to 820&nbsp;ms</td><td>positions and Status from 450 to 810&nbsp;ms</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Window 2</th><td>868.4&nbsp;MHz, channel B</td><td>830 to 1200&nbsp;ms</td><td>positions and Status from 830 to 990&nbsp;ms, callsign from 1000 to 1190&nbsp;ms</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/second" %>

  <p class="lede">Window 2 extends 200&nbsp;ms into the next second to receive FLARM-generation devices still transmitting there. ADS-L&rsquo;s direct slot, reserved for transmissions between aircraft, ends at 1000&nbsp;ms. Positions must finish by then. A burst spends 4.64&nbsp;ms on air. The latest permitted start leaves room for its booked air time and a 5&nbsp;ms allowance before its window closes. Bench measurements found a worst case of 5.5&nbsp;ms from the transmit command to the end of the burst, within that allowance.</p>
  <p class="lede">Each change between windows takes 10&nbsp;ms: 2&nbsp;ms still listening on the old channel for a late burst, 5&nbsp;ms to retune, then 3&nbsp;ms listening on the new channel before its window opens. Those 3&nbsp;ms also leave room to check the channel before a burst scheduled for the window&rsquo;s first instant.</p>

  <h3 id="channel">The parity of the second picks the channel.</h3>
  <p class="lede">A position uses channel A in an even UTC second and channel B in an odd one, both on the ground and in flight. A Status message reports what the Go can receive. It uses the channel opposite to the one a position would use in that second. The callsign always uses channel B, at the end of window 2.</p>
  <p class="lede">ADS-L requires consecutive positions to change channel, and consecutive Status messages to do the same. The even-or-odd rule makes the position channel predictable from the time alone. A receiver listening on only one channel can still receive every other scheduled position. At takeoff or landing, two positions in a row can use the same channel, but only once at the change between ground and flight. This happens at nearly one change in two: 45&nbsp;% of the takeoff and landing phases covered by the tests.</p>

  <h3 id="cycle">A 20&nbsp;s cycle that starts from the serial.</h3>
  <p class="lede">Each Go follows a 20&nbsp;s cycle. Its start, called c here, is an even second: twice the last decimal digit of its serial number, or <span class="mono">c = 2 &times; (serial mod 10)</span>. skyblip.eu assigns that number at <%= link_to "activation", briefing_activation_path %>. For the example unit <span class="mono">skyBlip Go 00012A</span>, the hexadecimal serial is 298 in decimal. Its last digit is 8, so its cycle starts at second 16.</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Last digit of the serial</th><th>The cycle starts at</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>0</th><td>:00, :20 and :40</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1</th><td>:02, :22 and :42</td></tr>
        <tr><th>2</th><td>:04, :24 and :44</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3</th><td>:06, :26 and :46</td></tr>
        <tr><th>4</th><td>:08, :28 and :48</td></tr>
        <tr><th>5</th><td>:10, :30 and :50</td></tr>
        <tr><th>6</th><td>:12, :32 and :52</td></tr>
        <tr><th>7</th><td>:14, :34 and :54</td></tr>
        <tr><th>8</th><td>:16, :36 and :56</td></tr>
        <tr><th>9</th><td>:18, :38 and :58</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">skyblip.eu assigns serials in order from 1, spreading consecutive units evenly over the ten cycle starts. Each unit schedules its bursts at the same offsets from its own start:</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Second</th><th>On the ground</th><th>In flight</th><th>Channel</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>c</th><td>position</td><td>position</td><td>A</td></tr>
        <tr><th>c + 2</th><td>Status</td><td>position and Status</td><td>Status on B, position on A</td></tr>
        <tr><th>c + 4</th><td>callsign</td><td>position and callsign</td><td>position on A, callsign on B with its start in the first 190&nbsp;ms of c + 5</td></tr>
        <tr><th>c + 9</th><td>position</td><td>position</td><td>B</td></tr>
        <tr><th>c + 11</th><td>Status</td><td>position and Status</td><td>Status on A, position on B</td></tr>
        <tr><th>c + 14</th><td>callsign</td><td>position and callsign</td><td>position on A, callsign on B with its start in the first 190&nbsp;ms of c + 15</td></tr>
        <tr><th>any other</th><td>nothing scheduled</td><td>position</td><td>A in an even second, B in an odd one</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/cycle" %>

  <p class="lede">On the ground, <span class="mono">skyBlip Go 00012A</span> schedules positions at :05, :16, :25, :36, :45 and :56 in each minute, Status messages at :07, :18, :27, :38, :47 and :58, and its callsign at the start of :01, :11, :21, :31, :41 and :51. In flight, it schedules a position every second. The Status and callsign schedule stays the same through takeoff and landing.</p>
  <p class="lede">An exhaustive search chose these offsets to meet four rules:</p>
  <ul class="points">
    <li>Ground positions are 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart. A gap of 10&nbsp;s would put consecutive positions on the same channel. The average remains one every 10&nbsp;s, skyBlip&rsquo;s reading of the ADS-L ground rate</li>
    <li>Positions and Status messages each alternate channels. Each channel carries a Status every 20&nbsp;s. The callsign goes every 10&nbsp;s</li>
    <li>The callsign is scheduled in an even second, when the position uses A and leaves B free for it</li>
    <li>The schedule keeps bursts from the same unit on the same frequency at least 100&nbsp;ms apart, with no more than one in each window</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="lede">The schedule also spreads ground positions and Status messages evenly. Across the ten cycle starts, each channel&rsquo;s window in each second carries one scheduled burst of either type from a unit on the ground. A Status on A always falls in an odd second. A Status on B always falls in an even second. That leaves even-second A windows and odd-second B windows for ground positions. Positions alternating between those windows must be an odd number of seconds apart. That is why ground positions stay 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart rather than exactly 10&nbsp;s. Positions 10&nbsp;s apart on alternating channels would put two ground bursts in a quarter of the windows and none in another quarter. At a busy airfield, those crowded windows would have more collisions.</p>
  <p class="lede">The schedule always uses the unit&rsquo;s own address, even when its messages carry an aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address. Setting or clearing that address in flight changes neither the timing nor the channel of a burst.</p>

  <h3 id="instant">The millisecond is drawn again every second.</h3>
  <p class="lede">Within its window, a position&rsquo;s start time comes from a hash: a calculation that mixes the unit&rsquo;s address with the UTC second and spreads the result across the available milliseconds. Status and callsign messages use further rounds of the same calculation, giving each its own draw. Units sharing a scheduled second need not share a start time. If their bursts overlap, the next second gives them new draws.</p>
  <p class="lede">For a 5&nbsp;ms burst spread over the 550&nbsp;ms direct slot, start times within 5&nbsp;ms of each other give a rough collision estimate of 1.8&nbsp;% per neighbour in range. Drawing again reduces the risk of the same pair colliding repeatedly. With a fixed instant per unit, two unlucky aircraft could overlap every second for the whole flight.</p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "radio-log.png", alt: "The radio log: below the satellite positioning status, the PPS timing signal state and the background noise level, one row per radio burst received or sent, newest first, with its time to the millisecond" %>
    <figcaption>The radio log lists each burst with its time to the millisecond</figcaption>
